{
  "configuration_id": "config_020",
  "reason": "Evidence: config_018 (multimodal, multiprofile, pca_n2000, resnet50, avgpool, concat) achieved the best result (acc 0.619, F1 0.610), showing a large ~9% gain over the singleprofile multimodal baseline (config_016, acc 0.527). However, we have only tested one aggregation method (avgpool) in the multiprofile setting. In the singleprofile comparison, concat vs weighted fusion made almost no difference (0.527 vs 0.523), suggesting fusion is a lower-priority axis. The aggregation mechanism is a more fundamental design choice: avgpool simply averages profile representations, while a transformer can model interactions between profiles. Question: Does transformer aggregation outperform avgpool when combining multiple profiles in a multimodal model, holding fusion=concat constant? Why this is the best next experiment: config_020 changes exactly one variable (aggregation: avgpool→transformer) relative to our current best (config_018), giving a clean isolation of the aggregation effect. If transformer helps substantially, it reveals that profile interactions are informative and justifies further investment in richer aggregation; if it does not, we confirm avgpool is sufficient and can redirect budget toward RNA encoder or image encoder choices. This is the highest-information single experiment available under the limited budget."
}

